% Goal
Implement hands/obs_control.py in Python over native OBS WebSocket v5 using
optional websocket-client. Expose OBSControlError for sanitized transport and
protocol failures and READ_ACTIONS, WRITE_ACTIONS, OUTPUT_ACTIONS frozensets.
No connection is created during import, construction or validation. The context
manager closes the connection and clears its password reference. The client
is synchronous, single-owner, reusable for sequential permitted execute calls;
close and any communication failure are terminal, not implicit retry triggers.

% Endpoint and protocol
Allow only 127.0.0.1, ::1 and localhost. Normalize localhost to literal 127.0.0.1
so host lookup cannot redirect credentials. Port is an integer 1..65535, not
boolean. Timeout is finite, positive, at most 300 seconds and not boolean.
Password is a string or None. Bypass HTTP proxies and prohibit redirects.
Hello op=0 must negotiate RPC>=1. Identify op=1 uses rpcVersion=1,
eventSubscriptions=0, and OBS's salted/challenged SHA256/base64 authentication
when Hello requests it. A challenge without a password fails; never disable
server authentication. Identified op=2 must negotiate exact integer RPC 1.
Requests use op=6 with fresh requestId, requestType and requestData. Receive
op=7 with matching requestId/type, success result exactly true and object
responseData. Ignore op=5 events only under the same monotonic operation
deadline. Malformed/mismatched/error responses close the client. Server comments
and raw exceptions are not returned. Use finite socket timeouts, no automatic
retries, and non-waiting close so shutdown cannot extend deadlines.
Each execute gets one absolute monotonic deadline covering literal-loopback TCP
connect, HTTP upgrade, OBS identification, every send/receive (including inner
partial-read/write loops and fragmented frames), aggregates and events. Recompute
remaining time before every low-level socket I/O; trickling bytes must not renew
the budget. A reused connection gets a fresh deadline only at the next execute.
Preconnect a deadline-aware literal-loopback socket and supply it to
websocket-client, bypassing DNS/proxy connection setup. Release the raw socket
if connect/upgrade fails; on client close release it without protocol I/O.

% Action schema
All fields listed below are required for direct Python execution; data omitted
means {}. Return a copied validated dict and reject any extra field. Flags are
strict booleans. Names are nonempty strings <=1024 characters without NUL.
Text is a string <=65536 characters without NUL. Numeric booleans are rejected.

Read actions:
- version -> GetVersion, scenes -> GetSceneList, inputs -> GetInputList: {}.
- status: {} -> stream GetStreamStatus, record GetRecordStatus, stats GetStats.
- scene-items: sceneName -> GetSceneItemList.
- audio: inputName -> mute GetInputMute and volume GetInputVolume.
- screenshot: sourceName, width, height -> GetSourceScreenshot with imageFormat
  png and imageWidth/imageHeight; dimensions are integers 8..4096. Return imageData
  for the caller to save; the CLI does not dump it into agent context.

Write actions (allow_write=True required):
- scene-create, scene-switch, scene-preview: sceneName -> CreateScene,
  SetCurrentProgramScene, SetCurrentPreviewScene respectively.
- source-visible: sceneName, sceneItemId (integer 0..2^31-1), sceneItemEnabled
  (boolean) -> SetSceneItemEnabled.
- input-mute: inputName, inputMuted (boolean) -> SetInputMute.
- input-volume: inputName, inputVolumeDb (finite -100..0) -> SetInputVolume.
- studio-mode: studioModeEnabled (boolean) -> SetStudioModeEnabled.
- text-set: inputName, text -> verify existing input kind through GetInputList,
  then SetInputSettings overlay=true and only {text}. Allowed kinds are
  text_ft2_source, text_ft2_source_v2, text_gdiplus, text_gdiplus_v2.
- color-create: sceneName, inputName, color (integer 0..2^32-1 ABGR), width,
  height (integers 1..4096) -> CreateInput color_source_v3 with only those
  inputSettings and sceneItemEnabled=true.

Output actions (both allow_write=True and allow_output=True required):
stream-start, stream-stop, record-start, record-stop with {} -> StartStream,
StopStream, StartRecord, StopRecord. Permission flags declare caller
human authorization; they are not an independent human-consent mechanism.

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): validate action names, schemas and permission flags before connecting or sending any request.
R2 (MUST NOT): connect to a non-loopback endpoint, follow redirects or route OBS credentials through a proxy.
R3 (MUST): negotiate OBS v5 authentication/RPC, match response IDs/types and enforce one total deadline per execute including aggregates and interleaved events.
R4 (MUST NOT): expose raw RPC, stream service settings, Twitch tokens, stream keys, arbitrary source settings, source deletion or arbitrary input creation.
R5 (MUST NOT): perform mutations without write permission or stream/record start/stop without separate output permission.
R6 (MUST NOT): retry a mutation after error, automatically start outputs, disable authentication, launch OBS, or change OBS preferences.
R7 (MUST): errors are fixed sanitized messages without passwords, server comments, input settings or raw transport details; failed communication closes the socket.
R8 (MUST): text updates apply only to an existing text source, never a browser/media source or nonexistent name.
R9 (MUST): reject malformed protocol objects and received messages larger than 16 MiB; close without waiting for peer acknowledgement.
R10 (MUST NOT): alter Mixxx timing, music files or personal plan state.
</contract_rules>

<vocabulary>
malformed: an OBS envelope missing the integer op or object d, a Hello/RPC value
outside the declared handshake, a response with mismatched ID/type or non-object
responseData, or JSON which cannot be decoded.
</vocabulary>

% Verification
Run .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_obs_control.py -v.
Use synthetic in-memory transport seams and real ephemeral-loopback RFC6455
fixtures. Cover authentication, schema/gates, all public action mappings,
text-source restrictions, failure sanitization, IDs, events, deadlines, local
endpoint normalization, screenshot limits and close. Never invoke real OBS
outputs in tests. Real OBS access is separately demonstrated by authorized
read-only version/status/scenes calls; synthetic fixtures do not prove it.
